+        ``sparse.index_ring_rows(rows)`` rows instead of ``capacity`` (1 KB a token for 11 layers: 1 GB per 1M
+        tokens -> ~2 MB). A row of them is read only when the window that completes its pool runs, so a ring
+        holding a window and the 3 rows before it serves every step with the same bits; the pool keys (every
+        complete pool, what scoring reads) stay full. The MTP layer's caches stay full (its positions follow the
+        head's own length). A snapshot must still bring back the last 3 committed rows (the incomplete pool) when
+        it rewinds past rows the ring overwrote: ``commit`` keeps them in a trailing slot of ``conv``, which every
+        snapshot already copies and restores, and ``set_pos`` writes them back on a rewind."""
